From: nix-wasm <eric@codebutler.com>
Date: Sat, 20 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: real fork() over the asyncify double-return seam (musl-fork)

Phase 2 (musl-fork variant only — canonical musl is unchanged). The NOMMU wasm
port cannot fork a running instance via SYS_clone, so fork() no longer issues the
clone syscall. Instead _Fork() arms a {cur,end} asyncify control buffer over a
static 16 KiB stack-image region and calls capture_stack(), a host import that
unwinds this instance's call stack to the runtime; the runtime duplicates the
address space + drives the kernel clone (wasm_fork_current) and REWINDS both this
instance and the child, so capture_stack() returns the child pid in the parent
and 0 in the child. __post_Fork() then runs once per side; the sig-block + abort
LOCK taken before the unwind are balanced by __post_Fork's UNLOCK + restore on
both sides (the child's copied memory resumes with them held — fork-time state).

capture_stack is the sole asyncify-imports unwind point, so wasm-opt --asyncify
(applied at the fork program's link by userspace/asyncify-cc.nix) instruments only
the fork call graph; the generic typed-syscall path stays asyncify-free, so
posix_spawn / vfork+exec (clone-with-fn) pay no asyncify cost. The control buffer
+ stack region live in BSS so they ride along in the runtime's verbatim memory
copy and the child rewinds the identical image.

Contract: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-20-fork-host-abi-v3.md.

__post_Fork TU split (precedes the Phase-2 fork-default flip): __post_Fork()
moves out of this file into a new src/process/post_Fork.c. Reason: musl upstream
co-defines __post_Fork() in _Fork.c because upstream only ever reaches it via a
real fork(). But src/linux/clone.c's clone_start() and clone() ALSO call
__post_Fork (to mimic _Fork()'s bookkeeping for a CLONE_VM-less clone()), and our
own harness patch (0000) additionally defines wasm's __clone() INSIDE clone.c
(upstream gives __clone its own TU) — so posix_spawn.c and pthread_create.c,
which reference __clone() and never fork(), pull in the WHOLE clone.lo object at
link time, including clone()'s two references to __post_Fork. With __post_Fork
still defined in _Fork.c, that reference dragged the entire _Fork.lo — and, in
this musl-fork variant, its capture_stack import — into every spawn-only or
thread-creating link, even though nothing on that path can ever call fork().
--export-all (wasm-cross.nix) roots every defined symbol, so --gc-sections can't
strip the now-unreachable-but-still-referenced _Fork/capture_stack import back
out; the coupling is structural (a link-graph fact), not something dead-code
elimination can fix. Left alone, this would mean "imports capture_stack" no
longer implies "the binary was asyncify-instrumented for fork" once the
Phase-2 default flip lands — a non-asyncified spawn/thread-only binary that
happens to link posix_spawn or pthread_create would carry the import too, so a
genuine, un-instrumented fork() call downstream would fail at RUNTIME with an
opaque TypeError (missing host import) instead of at LINK time, defeating the
whole point of gating fork on a link-time signal.

The fix: __post_Fork (+ its dummy/__aio_atfork weak-alias companions) now lives
in src/process/post_Fork.c. clone.lo's two __post_Fork references are satisfied
by that small companion object instead, so posix_spawn.c/pthread_create.c's pull
of __clone (hence clone.lo) no longer drags _Fork.lo along — _Fork.lo (and its
capture_stack import) is now pulled in ONLY when something actually references
_Fork()/fork(). This restores "imports capture_stack ⟺ can reach fork()" as a
real link-time contract. capture_stack is deliberately NOT added to the shared
--allow-undefined-file list in toolchain/wasm-host-imports.nix (the #52 no-undef
contract) — doing so would be exactly the wrong fix: it would make ANY binary
free to reference capture_stack without asyncify instrumentation, silently
trading a loud link failure for the same class of runtime TypeError this split
exists to prevent. A non-asyncified fork user must keep failing to link.

Scoped to the musl-fork variant only (this patch, 0010, is applied only when
`fork = true` in toolchain/musl.nix): in the default (fork = false) variant
_Fork.c is unmodified by this patch, fork()'s body is deleted from fork.c
(musl.nix postPatch), and _Fork() itself is called from nowhere else in the
tree — so the pre-split coupling exists there too (clone.lo still references
__post_Fork, defined in _Fork.c) but is inert: _Fork.c carries no capture_stack
import in that variant, so pulling it into a spawn/thread link is harmless dead
code, not a false "can fork" signal. The split is variant-independent in
principle (post_Fork.c compiles unchanged either way) and could move to the base
0000-0009 patch series later; it is kept here, scoped to fork = true, because
this is the only variant where the coupling is actually load-bearing today.

Duplicating a weak __aio_atfork fallback in post_Fork.c (alongside the existing
one in fork.c) mirrors musl's own status quo: upstream already carries the
identical dummy/weak_alias pair in BOTH fork.c and _Fork.c today, relying on
ordinary weak-symbol linking (a real __aio_atfork elsewhere overrides every
weak fallback). Moving the pair from _Fork.c to post_Fork.c keeps that same
two-copy shape; it does not introduce a new duplicate-definition hazard in
either variant.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
diff --git a/src/process/_Fork.c b/src/process/_Fork.c
index ac59b14..2338318 100644
--- a/src/process/_Fork.c
+++ b/src/process/_Fork.c
@@ -7,24 +7,42 @@
 #include "aio_impl.h"
 #include "fork_impl.h"

-static void dummy(int x) { }
-weak_alias(dummy, __aio_atfork);
+/*
+ * Phase 2 (musl-fork variant only): real fork() over the asyncify double-return
+ * seam. The NOMMU wasm port cannot fork a running instance via SYS_clone, so
+ * fork() does NOT issue the clone syscall — it calls capture_stack(), a host
+ * import that unwinds this instance's call stack to the runtime, which then
+ * duplicates the address space + drives the kernel clone (wasm_fork_current) and
+ * REWINDS both this instance and the child so capture_stack() returns the child
+ * pid here and 0 in the child. capture_stack is the sole asyncify-imports unwind
+ * point (the generic syscall path stays asyncify-free). The control buffer is a
+ * {cur,end} cursor over a static 16 KiB stack-image region; both live in BSS, so
+ * they ride along in the runtime's verbatim memory copy and the child rewinds
+ * the identical image. Contract: docs/.../2026-06-20-fork-host-abi-v3.md.
+ */
+extern int capture_stack(void *ctl);
+static struct { unsigned cur, end; } __wasm_fork_ctl;
+static char __wasm_fork_stack[16384] __attribute__((aligned(16)));

-void __post_Fork(int ret)
-{
-	if (!ret) {
-		pthread_t self = __pthread_self();
-		self->tid = __syscall(SYS_set_tid_address, &__thread_list_lock);
-		self->robust_list.off = 0;
-		self->robust_list.pending = 0;
-		self->next = self->prev = self;
-		__thread_list_lock = 0;
-		libc.threads_minus_1 = 0;
-		if (libc.need_locks) libc.need_locks = -1;
-	}
-	UNLOCK(__abort_lock);
-	if (!ret) __aio_atfork(1);
-}
+/*
+ * __post_Fork() (+ its dummy/__aio_atfork weak-alias companions) used to be
+ * defined HERE, but now lives in its own TU (src/process/post_Fork.c). musl
+ * upstream co-defines them in this file because upstream's __post_Fork is
+ * only ever reached via a real fork() — but src/linux/clone.c's clone_start()
+ * and clone() ALSO call __post_Fork (to mimic _Fork()'s bookkeeping for a
+ * CLONE_VM-less clone()), and our own harness patch (0000) additionally
+ * defines wasm's __clone() INSIDE clone.c (upstream gives it its own TU) —
+ * so posix_spawn.c and pthread_create.c, which call __clone(), pull in the
+ * WHOLE clone.lo object, including clone()'s reference to __post_Fork. With
+ * __post_Fork still defined in THIS file, that reference dragged _Fork.lo —
+ * and, in the musl-fork variant, its capture_stack import — into every
+ * spawn-only or thread-creating link, even though nothing on that path ever
+ * calls fork()/_Fork(). Splitting __post_Fork into post_Fork.c means
+ * clone.lo now satisfies its __post_Fork reference from that small companion
+ * instead, and _Fork.lo (hence capture_stack) is only pulled in when
+ * something actually references _Fork()/fork(). See post_Fork.c's header for
+ * the rest of the rationale.
+ */

 pid_t _Fork(void)
 {
@@ -32,18 +50,20 @@ pid_t _Fork(void)
 	sigset_t set;
 	__block_all_sigs(&set);
 	LOCK(__abort_lock);
-#ifdef SYS_fork
-	ret = __syscall(SYS_fork);
-#else
-	/* pc (linux-wasm #139): pad clone to the kernel's 5-arg arity. On wasm,
-	 * syscalls are typed indirect calls — a 2-arg call site into 5-arg
-	 * sys_clone (SYSCALL_DEFINE5) is a signature mismatch -> kernel-mode
-	 * trap -> "Aiee" panic. fork() was the last unpadded clone shortcut
-	 * (the clone() library path is already full-arity); Nix's builder
-	 * fork()s, so every in-guest derivation hit this. The extra ptid/tls/
-	 * ctid are 0 (no CLONE_*_SETTID/SETTLS set) so semantics are unchanged. */
-	ret = __syscall(SYS_clone, SIGCHLD, 0, 0, 0, 0);
-#endif
+	/*
+	 * Phase 2 seam (see the banner above). Arm the asyncify control buffer at
+	 * the base of the stack-image region, then unwind via capture_stack(). On the
+	 * unwind pass this RETURNS UP the stack (so the lines below don't run yet);
+	 * the runtime rewinds us, re-entering at this call site, where capture_stack
+	 * returns the child pid (parent) / 0 (child). __post_Fork then runs once per
+	 * side. The sig-block + LOCK above were taken on the unwind pass and are
+	 * balanced by __post_Fork's UNLOCK + __restore_sigs below on BOTH sides (the
+	 * child's copied memory resumes with them held — fork-time state).
+	 */
+	__wasm_fork_ctl.cur = (unsigned)(unsigned long)__wasm_fork_stack;
+	__wasm_fork_ctl.end = (unsigned)(unsigned long)__wasm_fork_stack
+		+ sizeof __wasm_fork_stack;
+	ret = capture_stack(&__wasm_fork_ctl);
 	__post_Fork(ret);
 	__restore_sigs(&set);
 	return __syscall_ret(ret);
diff --git a/src/process/post_Fork.c b/src/process/post_Fork.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e5ebb26
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/process/post_Fork.c
@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+#include <unistd.h>
+#include <signal.h>
+#include "syscall.h"
+#include "libc.h"
+#include "lock.h"
+#include "pthread_impl.h"
+#include "aio_impl.h"
+#include "fork_impl.h"
+
+/*
+ * Split out of _Fork.c (musl-fork variant, patch 0010) so that clone.c's two
+ * callers of __post_Fork() — clone_start() and clone() itself — can be
+ * satisfied WITHOUT pulling in _Fork.lo (and, on wasm, its capture_stack
+ * import). See the banner in _Fork.c for the full rationale: __clone() lives
+ * in clone.c too (our harness patch 0000 put it there), so posix_spawn.c and
+ * pthread_create.c — which reference __clone() and never fork() — used to
+ * drag capture_stack into their link purely via this symbol's old home.
+ *
+ * This TU is otherwise byte-identical to upstream's __post_Fork (content,
+ * not just behavior) — only its file changed. Duplicating a weak
+ * __aio_atfork fallback here (alongside the one in fork.c) mirrors musl's
+ * own existing practice: upstream already carries the identical pair in
+ * BOTH fork.c and _Fork.c (a real fork() build links harmless duplicate
+ * weak definitions, and the linker's own weak-symbol resolution is what
+ * lets a real __aio_atfork elsewhere override every one of them) — moving
+ * it from _Fork.c to post_Fork.c keeps that same two-copy shape, it just
+ * relocates one of the two copies.
+ */
+static void dummy(int x) { }
+weak_alias(dummy, __aio_atfork);
+
+void __post_Fork(int ret)
+{
+	if (!ret) {
+		pthread_t self = __pthread_self();
+		self->tid = __syscall(SYS_set_tid_address, &__thread_list_lock);
+		self->robust_list.off = 0;
+		self->robust_list.pending = 0;
+		self->next = self->prev = self;
+		__thread_list_lock = 0;
+		libc.threads_minus_1 = 0;
+		if (libc.need_locks) libc.need_locks = -1;
+	}
+	UNLOCK(__abort_lock);
+	if (!ret) __aio_atfork(1);
+}
